The Analyze tab's tool gallery and the Tools pane running Buffer. <p>Open a tool from Analyze › Portal › Feature Analysis or Raster Analysis, Analyze › Tools (the Tools pane, which searches every tool) or Command Search. Fill in its parameters and click Run: new layers or tables are added to the project; selection tools select existing rows and Calculate Geometry Attributes updates the input layer. Each run is listed under Analyze › History. From the command line or a script (<code>gp.run</code>), a built-in tool's parameter with a list of choices, such as Buffer's units, takes one of them in any letter case (<code>"meters"</code> for <code>Meters</code>); any other value is an error that names the choices (Buffer's units no longer take the abbreviations <code>m</code>, <code>mi</code> or <code>metres</code>). Buffer and Select Layer By Location accept Kilometers, Meters, Miles and Feet. Feet are international feet (exactly 0.3048 metres): a 500-foot notification radius is 152.4 metres on the ground.</p> <p>The History pane lists each run with when it ran (in UTC). Its ⋯ menu, or a right-click on the tool's name, copies a run as code, with the parameters it ran with: Copy as Python (a call for the <code>rustgis</code> package, one parameter a line), Copy as Command (a line for the Script pane) and Copy as CLI Command (<code>rustgis-cli run --project … --cmd …</code>, quoted for a POSIX shell: macOS, Linux, WSL or Git Bash; on Windows' own shells, use the Python or the Script line). Open in Tool opens the tool's form with those values, to change and run again. Save as Python Script… writes the whole history as a script that opens the project and runs each tool in order; a run that failed is there as a comment. The CLI command and the script open the project's file: for a project not saved yet, they name the file it would be saved as, so save it there first; and a project changed since it was saved needs saving again, or the script opens it without those changes (a layer added since, say). What gives access is taken out of the code: addresses lose their keys, tokens and passwords, a connection its password, a pasted private key goes, and a parameter named as a password, key, secret or token is left empty (<code>gp.historyCode</code> and <code>gp.historyScript</code> do the same from a script). A secret written some other way (a token typed into a SQL query, say) can still be there: read the code before sharing it. A point buffered where the map was clicked is copied as the command that buffered it, with the point.</p> <p>These are the built-in tools; GeoLibre and plugin tools join them when available. Use these ids in <code>gp.run</code>'s <code>tool</code> parameter (<code>gp.tools</code> lists the available tools and their parameters):</p> Tool Id Toolbox Buffer <code>buffer</code> Analysis › Proximity Near <code>near</code> Analysis › Proximity Clip <code>clip</code> Analysis › Extract Split <code>split</code> Analysis › Extract Erase <code>erase</code> Analysis › Overlay Intersect <code>intersect</code> Analysis › Overlay Identity <code>identity</code> Analysis › Overlay Union <code>union</code> Analysis › Overlay Symmetrical Difference <code>symmetricalDifference</code> Analysis › Overlay Update <code>update</code> Analysis › Overlay Spatial Join <code>spatialJoin</code> Analysis › Overlay Join Field <code>joinField</code> Data Management › Joins Dissolve <code>dissolve</code> Data Management › Generalization Minimum Bounding Geometry <code>convexHull</code> Data Management › Features Feature To Point <code>featureToPoint</code> Data Management › Features Merge <code>merge</code> Data Management › General Feature Vertices To Points <code>featureVerticesToPoints</code> Data Management › Features XY Table To Point <code>xyTableToPoint</code> Data Management › Features Calculate Geometry Attributes <code>calculateGeometry</code> Data Management › Features Simplify <code>simplify</code> Cartography › Generalization Tissot's Indicatrix <code>tissot</code> Cartography › Projections A5 Grid <code>a5Grid</code> Analysis › DGGS Aggregate Points to A5 <code>a5Aggregate</code> Analysis › DGGS Select Layer By Location <code>selectByLocation</code> Data Management › Layers and Table Views Select Layer By Attribute <code>selectByAttributes</code> Data Management › Layers and Table Views Summarize Within <code>countPoints</code> Analysis › Summarize Summary Statistics <code>summaryStatistics</code> Analysis › Statistics Pivot Table <code>pivotTable</code> Analysis › Statistics Export Features <code>exportFeatures</code> Conversion Vector to PMTiles <code>vectorToPmtiles</code> Analyze › Tools Raster to PMTiles <code>rasterToPmtiles</code> Analyze › Tools IDW Interpolation <code>idwInterpolation</code> Analysis › Interpolation Ordinary Kriging <code>kriging</code> Analysis › Interpolation Zonal Statistics <code>zonalStats</code> Analysis › Raster Kernel Density <code>kernelDensity</code> Analysis › Density <p>Raster to PMTiles (<code>rasterToPmtiles</code>) writes Web Mercator PNG tiles using the raster's current bands, colors, mask and resampling. Set the minimum and maximum zoom from 0 to 16 and choose a <code>.pmtiles</code> output; without one it uses the last component of the layer name. Layer and group transparency are included. The raster must be georeferenced and fit within one world. Turn off dynamic range adjustment and display rotation first. The in-memory export is limited to 512 tiles and 64 MiB of compressed tile data; reduce the extent or zoom range if it exceeds a limit. Areas beyond the Web Mercator latitude limit are clipped.</p> <p>Vector to PMTiles (<code>vectorToPmtiles</code>) writes a single-layer MVT archive in standard Web Mercator. Choose <code>minZoom</code> and <code>maxZoom</code> from 0 to 16; <code>sourceLayer</code> defaults to the input layer's name. The output file is saved atomically on desktop or downloaded in the browser. Without <code>output</code>, CLI, script and task calls write <code>&lt;layer&gt;.pmtiles</code> in the process's working directory, where <code>&lt;layer&gt;</code> is the layer's name without any folder part; an existing destination is replaced atomically. The desktop tool pane instead asks for a destination with Save As. Native exports refuse to replace the open/saved project file, the package this project was opened from, or a file that a loaded layer, raster, standalone table or tile archive reads. For directory-backed sources such as File Geodatabases, files inside the source directory are protected as well. This includes path aliases, ZIP archives holding loaded members, and Shapefile companion files. Project/package paths stay anchored across changes to the working directory, and their origins are restored when a project replacement is undone or redone. Browser downloads cannot check the eventual save destination; choose a different file in the browser's save dialog to avoid replacing an input. An active selection is exported; otherwise the geoprocessing extent applies, and streamed sources are read in full rather than limited to the visible map window. It exports points, lines and polygons, including multipart geometry, attributes and feature IDs—not the map's style or labels. Antimeridian paths are unwrapped and clipped with an 8/4096-tile buffer; latitudes beyond Web Mercator's limit are clamped and reported. Geometry parts collapsed by integer quantization and features without geometry are reported. Null properties are omitted; integer attributes outside ±2^53 are rejected to avoid precision loss.</p> <p>This is an in-memory export, limited to 10,000 distinct output tiles and a conservative 256 MiB working-memory budget, including the input, clipping/encoding temporaries and final archive. This budget is checked after streamed-source materialization, not during provider loading: the existing full-read limits (2,000,000 features on desktop, 500,000 in the browser) apply first, and loading may use more than 256 MiB before export refuses the input. Reduce a large source before loading it for export. Geometry allocation estimates are linear in the number of vertices and rings. Each geometry component's buffered candidate rectangle is limited to 40,000 tiles per zoom, including candidates where clipping produces no output. A separate 100,000,000-unit geometry-work budget accumulates across all exported features, components and zooms: each candidate tile costs the component's vertex count, and each polygon validity check (before clipping and after integer quantization) costs <code>(vertices + rings)²</code>. The extra vertex per ring allows for implicit ring closure. The work-limit error is distinct from the memory-limit error: simplify polygon rings, reduce the input extent, or narrow the zoom range. These limits reject the whole export rather than sampling or truncating it. Browser task, model and script runs can queue at most eight files or 256 MiB of archive bytes before downloads are submitted; split larger batches into separate runs. Browser success means the download was submitted, not confirmation that the browser saved it. Example:</p> <pre><code>gp.run={"tool":"vectorToPmtiles","params":{"input":"Roads","minZoom":0,"maxZoom":12,"sourceLayer":"roads","output":"roads.pmtiles"}}
</code></pre> <p>Simplify uses Douglas–Peucker with a tolerance in kilometres. Each polygon ring keeps at least three different, non-collinear corners (plus the closing vertex), so a tolerance larger than a polygon produces a small ring instead of restoring its original vertices. Holes and multipart rings are retained. This is not a topology-preserving simplifier: check the result where holes or neighbouring polygons must stay aligned. The default tolerance remains 25 km; use a smaller value for local boundaries.</p> <p>Kernel Density (<code>kernelDensity</code>) calculates a continuous magnitude-per-unit-area raster surface from point features using a Quartic (biweight, default) or Gaussian kernel (truncated at 3σ and renormalised to integrate to 1). Points can optionally be weighted by a numeric population field (invalid or non-positive weights are excluded and reported in the tool messages; for multipoint features, each constituent point part receives the feature's weight). Calculations are performed in the linear units of a projected coordinate system (defaults to the layer's projected system; if the input layer lacks a coordinate system or is in degrees, or to project into a specific system, supply a projected system via <code>crs</code>, e.g. <code>EPSG:32618</code>). Because bandwidth and cell size are measured in coordinate-space linear units, global cylindrical projections such as Web Mercator (EPSG:3857) introduce scale distortion that increases with latitude; use a local projected coordinate system (such as UTM or State Plane) when accurate ground distances are required. Leaving <code>radius</code> empty or setting it to <code>0</code> automatically estimates the bandwidth using Silverman's rule of thumb based on the points' spatial distribution (falling back to extent span / 30, or 1000 coordinate-space linear units for a single point or coincident points). When <code>pixelSize</code> is omitted, an adaptive cell size is calculated to span approximately 250 cells along the longer side of the extent; the default search radius is automatically adjusted if needed to be at least the pixel size so points are not missed between cell centres. The search radius must be at least the pixel size. The extent can optionally be overridden via <code>extent</code> as <code>xmin,ymin,xmax,ymax</code> text or a four-number array. The output is written as a single-band Float32 GeoTIFF.</p> <p>Most feature tools process only the selected rows when an input has a selection. Selection tools instead replace the input's selection; Select Layer By Location honours the selecting layer's selection. Summarize Within counts the selected points against all input polygons, and Calculate Geometry Attributes updates all rows. For tools that create a map layer, <code>output</code> is the layer name; <code>vectorToPmtiles</code> uses it as a file destination.</p> <p>Buffer distances, and the distance of Select Layer By Location, are measured on the Earth (the WGS 84 ellipsoid, as PROJ and GeographicLib measure it), not in the map's display projection. Each feature is buffered in an azimuthal equidistant projection on the ellipsoid centred on it, so distances from a point are true; long lines and large polygon outlines are buffered in stretches of a few hundred kilometres to limit projection error. Up to 85° of latitude, a buffer's sides are within 0.012 % of the distance (or 1 cm), and within 0.05 % along a winding line or outline buffered in stretches. Its curves are drawn with straight edges (32 to a point's circle, 15 to a half circle at a line's end or a polygon's corner, close to GEOS and PostGIS's 8 to a quarter circle), whose corners are at the distance and whose middles run up to about 0.5 % of it inside the true curve. Negative Buffer distances shrink polygons. Buffer and Select Layer By Location limit distances to 5,000 km. Calculate Geometry Attributes measures polygon areas and perimeters, and line lengths, on the WGS 84 ellipsoid, independently of the map's display projection. Holes are subtracted regardless of ring winding, and multipart measurements are summed. Ring directions are normalized in continuous longitude space, so non-polar regions larger than half the Earth keep their full area and small antimeridian crossings stay small. Rings winding around a pole use the smaller polar region in either direction; reversing a ring does not select its global complement. Edges follow the shortest geodesics between vertices. The output fields remain <code>area_km2</code>, <code>perimeter_km</code> and <code>length_km</code>; point coordinates are unchanged. Spatial Join's Largest overlap still measures area on a sphere of radius 6,371 km; it can differ from the ellipsoid by up to about 0.9 % (a small area near a pole).</p> <p>Merge (<code>merge</code>, Data Management › General) appends loaded vector layers of the same geometry type (points, lines or polygons) into a new layer. It needs no GeoLibre runner. Choose Input Layers and Preserve Attributes in the tool pane, or pass names separated by <code>;</code> or a JSON array. Desktop runs can also name local vector files without adding them to the map first. In the browser, load the files as layers first. Each layer contributes its selection, or all its features when it has no selection; read-in-place layers without a selection contribute their complete source.</p> <pre><code>{"tool":"merge","params":{"inputs":["Survey A","Survey B"],"attributes":true,"output":"Combined survey"}}
</code></pre> <p>Matching field names (case-insensitive) and types share a column. Other fields are added and missing values are empty. Incompatible types remain separate columns with unique suffixes, such as <code>count_2</code>, preserving their original values and types. Turn off Preserve Attributes for geometry only. Multipart geometry and holes are kept unchanged, new feature ids are assigned, and export defaults to the first input's coordinate system; internal coordinates stay longitude and latitude. The inputs stay unchanged, and the new layer can be undone.</p> <p>Feature Vertices To Points (<code>featureVerticesToPoints</code>) writes a point at each vertex of the <code>input</code> layer's features (a point layer's points are their own vertices, and a multipoint's points its parts), with its feature's attributes and four fields. <code>orig_fid</code> is the feature's OID, linking each point to its feature as ArcGIS's tool of the same name does. Three say where the point lies, named as in QGIS's Extract Vertices: <code>vertex_part</code> (the part of a multipart feature), <code>vertex_part_ring</code> (the polygon ring: 0 for the outer ring, and for lines and points) and <code>vertex_part_index</code> (the place along the part or ring, from 0). Unlike QGIS's tool, it doesn't write a polygon ring's closing point, which repeats its first. A new field whose name the input already has gets a suffix (<code>vertex_part_2</code>). The points keep the input's coordinate system. In the web app, the tool stops with a message if the browser can't give it the points' memory: select fewer features, or simplify ones with many vertices.</p> <pre><code>gp.run={"tool":"featureVerticesToPoints","params":{"input":"Rivers","output":"river_vertices"}}
</code></pre> <p>IDW Interpolation works like the QGIS tool of the same name: every point weighs on every cell by 1/distanceᴾ, where P is the distance coefficient (2 by default). Lines and polygons contribute their vertices. Leave the extent empty to use the input's, or give it as <code>xmin,ymin,xmax,ymax</code> in degrees. <code>pixelSize</code> sets the cell size in degrees; when omitted it starts at 300 cells across the extent and coarsens for large inputs. The result is a Float32 GeoTIFF layer. The Geostatistical Wizard is a separate IDW workflow: it uses 12 nearest neighbours by default (<code>neighbors</code>), rather than every point.</p> <p>Ordinary Kriging (<code>kriging</code>) estimates both a value and its uncertainty. Set <code>input</code> and a numeric <code>field</code>, then choose a <code>model</code>: Spherical (default), Exponential or Gaussian. <code>neighbours</code> defaults to 12 (minimum 2, capped at 32). Optional <code>nugget</code>, <code>sill</code> and <code>range</code> override the defaults: zero nugget, the sample variance (1 for near-constant values), and a third of the extent's diagonal. Coordinates, <code>range</code>, <code>extent</code> and <code>pixelSize</code> are in degrees (WGS 84). Points at the same location are averaged; multipoints contribute their points, while lines and polygons contribute their bounding-box centres. At least two distinct locations with numeric values are needed. It adds two separate Float32 GeoTIFF layers: the prediction, named by <code>output</code>, and <code>&lt;output&gt;_variance</code>.</p> <p>Erase (<code>erase</code>) removes from <code>input</code> features (points, lines or polygons) their parts inside the <code>erase</code> polygons. An erase polygon's edge counts as inside it, as for Clip, so a point on the edge or a stretch of line along it is erased; features left with nothing are left out, and one no erase polygon overlaps is kept as it is.</p> <p>Intersect (<code>intersect</code>) splits <code>input</code> features by the polygons of <code>overlay</code>: each part of an input feature inside an overlay polygon becomes a feature with the attributes of both, the input's first. A parcel crossed by two flood zones gives two features, one for each zone, and a point in two overlapping polygons gives two. Input features outside every overlay polygon are left out. An overlay polygon's edge counts as inside it, as for Clip: a point on the edge and a stretch of line along it are kept, but polygons that only share an edge or a corner, and a line that only touches one, give nothing. The input can be points, lines or polygons and keeps its geometry type; the overlay must be polygons. An overlay field whose name the input already has gets a suffix (<code>id_2</code>). Overlapping features within one layer are not split against each other. Like Clip and Erase, the overlay works on the map's Web Mercator coordinates, so the parts' edges are straight on the map. Selections in both layers are honoured. To measure each part, run Calculate Geometry Attributes on the result:</p> <pre><code>gp.run={"tool":"intersect","params":{"input":"parcels","overlay":"floodplain","output":"parcels_in_floodplain"}}
</code></pre> <p>Identity (<code>identity</code>) takes the same parameters. Each input feature gives its Intersect features, then its part outside every overlay polygon, with empty overlay attributes. Every parcel is then in the result, in or out of the floodplain: one no overlay polygon meets, or without geometry, as it is (Intersect leaves those out). Union (<code>union</code>) takes two polygon layers and adds, after Identity's features, each overlay polygon's part outside every input polygon, with empty input attributes: together the features cover everything either layer covers. Both follow Intersect's rules for field names, touching parts and selections. Where overlay polygons overlap each other, an input feature's part in both comes out once for each, and Union adds each overlay polygon's own remainder, so the overlay's overlap outside the input comes out once for each too.</p> <p>Symmetrical Difference (<code>symmetricalDifference</code>) takes two polygon layers, as Union does, and keeps only the pieces one layer covers: Union's features without Intersect's. Each input feature's part outside every overlay polygon comes first, with empty overlay attributes, then each overlay polygon's part outside every input polygon, with empty input attributes. As in Union, an input feature without geometry is kept as it is, with empty overlay attributes, so no input row is lost.</p> <p>Update (<code>update</code>) replaces part of a polygon layer with another: each <code>input</code> polygon's part outside every <code>update</code> polygon keeps its attributes, and each update polygon is then added whole. The output has the input's fields only. An update polygon's values go into the input's fields of the same name (matched exactly, else ignoring case; converted to the field's type, so text that isn't a number becomes empty in a numeric field); the input's other fields are left empty. An input field that no update field matches exactly, but two match ignoring case, stops the tool rather than pick one. An input polygon no update polygon overlaps, or one without geometry, is kept as it is. Update polygons that overlap each other each come out whole, so their overlap comes out twice.</p> <pre><code>gp.run={"tool":"update","params":{"input":"parcels","update":"resurveyed_parcels"}}
</code></pre> <p>Split (<code>split</code>) clips <code>input</code> (points, lines or polygons) by each polygon of <code>split</code>, as Clip does, and adds one layer for each. Give a <code>field</code> of the split layer to name the layers by its values: polygons sharing a value make one layer together, and one without a value is named by its id. Without a field, each polygon gives a layer named by its id. Names start with <code>output</code>, else the input's name, and an underscore: <code>roads_North</code> (a name the map already has gets a number, as for any new layer). A feature on the edge between two split polygons goes into both layers. Layers that would be empty are left out. Split stops, before clipping anything, when there are more than 1,000 split areas (split polygons, or values of the field), empty ones included. Selections in both layers are honoured.</p> <pre><code>gp.run={"tool":"split","params":{"input":"roads","split":"districts","field":"name"}}
</code></pre> <p>Near (<code>near</code>) copies <code>input</code> and adds, for each feature, the nearest feature of <code>near</code>: its OID as the attribute table shows it (<code>near_fid</code>), the distance to it (<code>near_dist</code>, in <code>units</code>, Kilometers by default) and the point on it nearest the input feature (<code>near_x</code> and <code>near_y</code>, in longitude from −180° up to 180° and latitude). Distances are measured as Spatial Join's Closest measures them: on the ground, between the features' nearest points, and zero for features that intersect, whose point is one they share. The point is found as the distance is, in a plane centred between the features, so on a long edge it can sit off the geodesic by up to about 0.01 % of the distance (5 m at 50 km). A <code>distance</code> limits how far it looks; features with nothing that near, or without geometry, get empty values. Of equally near features, the first in the near layer wins. With the same layer as both, each feature's nearest is another feature, not itself. A field name the input already has gets a suffix (<code>near_dist_2</code>). Selections in both layers are honoured:</p> <pre><code>gp.run={"tool":"near","params":{"input":"addresses","near":"hydrants","units":"Feet"}}
</code></pre> <p>Spatial Join (<code>spatialJoin</code>, also <code>gp.spatialJoin</code>) copies attributes from <code>join</code> features to <code>target</code> features. Layers may have different source coordinate systems: their geometry is stored in longitude/latitude before the join. <code>match</code> is one of:</p> <ul> <li>Intersects (default), Contains (target contains join) or Within (target lies within   join).</li> <li>Have their center in: the join features holding the target's interior point, the point   Feature To Point gives. A parcel a zone boundary crosses goes with the zone its point falls in.</li> <li>Largest overlap: the one join polygon covering most of the target, by ground area for a   polygon target and ground length for a line. Polygons that only touch don't overlap. Equal   overlaps keep the join layer's order.</li> <li>Closest: the one nearest join feature. A <code>distance</code> (in <code>units</code>, as for Buffer; Kilometers   by default) limits how far it looks, leaving targets with nothing that near unmatched; without   one, every target gets its nearest feature. Ties keep the join layer's order.</li> <li>Within a distance: every join feature within <code>distance</code> (required), nearest first.</li> </ul> <p>Distances are on the ground (the WGS 84 ellipsoid, as for Buffer), between the features' nearest points (edges and vertices alike), and are zero for features that intersect. <code>distanceField</code> names a field added for the distance to the joined feature, in <code>units</code>; it applies to Closest and Within a distance only, as <code>distance</code> does, and the Tools pane shows them only for those.</p> <p>Join one to one (the default <code>operation</code>) copies the first matching feature's attributes; Join one to many makes a row for each match. Both add <code>join_count</code>, the total matches for that target (one at most for Largest overlap and Closest), and prefix joined fields with <code>join_</code> (change <code>prefix</code> to override it). <code>keepAll</code> defaults to Keep all, leaving unmatched joined values null; choose Keep only matching to drop those targets. Selections in both layers are honoured.</p> <p>In a one-to-one join, <code>merge</code> combines the values of all the matches instead of taking the first: a list such as <code>[{"field": "units", "rule": "sum"}, {"field": "name", "rule": "join", "delimiter": "; "}]</code> naming join fields. The rules are <code>first</code> (the default for fields without a rule), <code>last</code>, <code>sum</code> and <code>mean</code> (numeric fields), <code>min</code> and <code>max</code> (numbers or text), <code>count</code> (the matches with a value) and <code>join</code> (the values as text, separated by <code>delimiter</code>, <code>,</code> by default). Every rule but <code>first</code> and <code>last</code> skips empty values. A target without matches gets empty values, except a <code>count</code> of 0. <code>mean</code> writes a decimal field, <code>count</code> a whole number and <code>join</code> text. The <code>sum</code> of a whole-number field adds its whole numbers, and is empty when too large to hold. For example, the total units permitted in each district:</p> <pre><code>gp.run={"tool":"spatialJoin","params":{"target":"districts","join":"permits","merge":[{"field":"units","rule":"sum"}]}}
</code></pre> <p>In the Tools pane, use Add rule under Merge Rules, pick a field from Join Features and its rule, and set Delimiter for <code>join</code>. Use Move up, Move down and Remove to manage the list. Fields without a rule keep the first match.</p> <p>Join Field (<code>joinField</code>) performs a one-off non-spatial attribute join, copying fields from a source table or layer (<code>joinTable</code>, specified by name or numeric table ID) into a <code>target</code> table or layer based on matching key values:</p> <ul> <li>Key fields: <code>targetField</code> (aliases: <code>target_field</code>, <code>key</code>) names the key field in the target.   <code>joinField</code> (aliases: <code>join_field</code>) names the matching key in the source table; when omitted, empty,   or whitespace-only, it defaults to <code>targetField</code>. Numeric keys match across integer and floating-point   types when whole numbers (e.g. <code>10</code> matches <code>10.0</code>), while non-finite floats (<code>NaN</code>, infinity) and   nulls never match. Incompatible key data types (e.g. text vs integer) are rejected.</li> <li>Transferred fields: <code>fields</code> selects specific source fields to transfer (a comma-separated list   or a JSON array of strings, e.g. <code>["tax_rate", "zone_code"]</code>; non-string and non-array types are rejected). Repeated   field names are deduplicated in order. When omitted or passed as a blank string (<code>""</code>), every source field except   the join key is transferred; passing an explicitly empty array (<code>[]</code>) or an array of blank strings transfers zero fields.   Transferred fields preserve their original source display aliases (retained without technical prefixes) and field types.</li> <li>Prefix and collision handling: <code>prefix</code> prepends text to all transferred field names. When a   transferred field name conflicts with an existing target field, an incremental suffix is added   (<code>name_2</code>, <code>name_3</code>) to maintain schema uniqueness without overwriting existing data.</li> <li>Duplicate keys: <code>duplicates</code> (<code>duplicateStrategy</code>) determines what happens when multiple source   rows share the same join key: First (default: uses the first matching source record), Last   (uses the last matching source record), or Error (fails the operation and reports the duplicate key).</li> <li>Selections and streamed inputs: selections on the target layer or source table are honoured;   when a target selection is active, the output contains the selected target rows, while unselected   datasets are processed in full. Streamed layers that are only partially loaded in the view window are   rejected with a clear message (<code>the target layer is streamed; load all features first</code>) so incomplete   results are never committed as a whole layer. Rows with attribute counts exceeding the schema definition   are rejected with a clear error rather than silently truncated. Unmatched target records receive null values.</li> </ul> <pre><code>gp.run={"tool":"joinField","params":{"target":"parcels","targetField":"owner_id","joinTable":"owners","fields":["name","phone"],"duplicates":"First"}}
</code></pre> <p>Summary Statistics (<code>summaryStatistics</code>) makes a standalone table of statistics: one row for the whole table (none for an empty one), or one for each combination of values of the <code>case</code> fields (one field, or several as a list such as <code>["county", "year"]</code>). Rows are sorted by those values; records with no value form a group of their own, first. Statistics follow record order (<code>FIRST</code>, <code>LAST</code>, <code>CONCATENATE</code>) and use only the selected records when the table has a selection. Each row has the case fields, then <code>FREQUENCY</code> (the group's records), then a field <code>&lt;STAT&gt;_&lt;field&gt;</code> for each statistic asked for. Name the statistics in <code>statistics</code>, a list of up to 256 (field, statistic) pairs in the order the fields should appear:</p> <pre><code>gp.run={"tool":"summaryStatistics","params":{"input":"parcels","case":["county","year"],"statistics":[{"field":"income","statistic":"MEAN"},{"field":"income","statistic":"MEDIAN"},{"field":"zoning","statistic":"MODE"}]}}
</code></pre> <p>In the Tools pane, use Add statistic to pick each field and statistic. The statistic picker offers the operations supported by that field's type, including percentiles <code>P1</code> through <code>P99</code> for numeric fields. Use Add field under Case Fields to group by several fields. Move entries up or down to choose their output order, or remove them. Leaving Statistics empty keeps the single Statistics Field shortcut. These lists are also available in ModelBuilder; for an input produced by another tool, type its field names before running the model.</p> Statistic What it is <code>COUNT</code> Values that aren't empty <code>SUM</code>, <code>MEAN</code>, <code>MIN</code>, <code>MAX</code> The usual; <code>MIN</code> and <code>MAX</code> of text compare by character code, so ISO dates order as dates <code>RANGE</code> <code>MAX</code> − <code>MIN</code> <code>STD</code>, <code>VARIANCE</code> Population standard deviation and variance (divided by the count) <code>MEDIAN</code> The middle value; for an even count, the mean of the middle two <code>P1</code> to <code>P99</code> Percentiles, interpolated linearly between ranks (as a spreadsheet's <code>PERCENTILE.INC</code>), so <code>P50</code> is the median <code>IQR</code> <code>P75</code> − <code>P25</code> <code>CV</code> Coefficient of variation, <code>STD</code> / <code>MEAN</code>; empty when the mean is 0 <code>MODE</code>, <code>MINORITY</code> The most and least common value; on a tie, the lowest <code>UNIQUE</code> The number of distinct values <code>FIRST</code>, <code>LAST</code> The first and last value, in record order <code>CONCATENATE</code> The values in record order, joined by <code>separator</code> (<code>,</code> by default; 256 bytes at most) <code>NULLS</code> Records with no value <p>Every statistic but <code>NULLS</code> leaves out empty values. A text field takes <code>COUNT</code>, <code>MIN</code>, <code>MAX</code>, <code>FIRST</code>, <code>LAST</code>, <code>UNIQUE</code>, <code>CONCATENATE</code>, <code>MODE</code>, <code>MINORITY</code> and <code>NULLS</code>; the rest need a numeric field. A group with no values gets empty statistics, except a <code>COUNT</code> and <code>UNIQUE</code> of 0 (its <code>NULLS</code> is its <code>FREQUENCY</code>). Two output fields can't share a name, so a case field can't be called <code>FREQUENCY</code> or <code>&lt;STAT&gt;_&lt;field&gt;</code> for a statistic asked for. Counts are whole numbers, <code>CONCATENATE</code> and the statistics of a text field are text, and the others are decimal. Without <code>statistics</code>, <code>field</code> gives that field's <code>COUNT</code>, <code>SUM</code>, <code>MEAN</code>, <code>MIN</code>, <code>MAX</code> and <code>STD</code>, reading its values as numbers.</p> <p>Pivot Table (<code>pivotTable</code>) turns the values of one field into fields of their own. It makes a standalone table with a row for each combination of values of the row <code>fields</code> (one field, or several as a list); with no row fields there is one row for the whole table, or none for an empty table. Then it adds a field for each value of the <code>pivot</code> field, sorted by value. Each cell holds the <code>value</code> field's value for that row and pivot value, or is empty when no record has them. A table with a record per county and year becomes one with a row per county and a field per year:</p> <pre><code>gp.run={"tool":"pivotTable","params":{"input":"incomes","fields":"county","pivot":"year","value":"income"}}
</code></pre> county year2020 year2021 year2022 A 10 11 B 20 22 <p>Use Add field under Row Fields in the Tools pane or ModelBuilder to choose several row fields and order them with Move up and Move down.</p> <p>Without <code>statistic</code>, each row and pivot value can have one record at most; the tool names the first pair with more, and the cells keep the value field's type. Give a <code>statistic</code> to combine the records instead, such as the mean income by county and year from a table of households. It takes any statistic from Summary Statistics (above), with the same rules for text fields, empty values and output types; <code>separator</code> joins <code>CONCATENATE</code>'s values. A cell with no records is empty even for <code>COUNT</code>, which tells it apart from records that have no value (a <code>COUNT</code> of 0):</p> <pre><code>gp.run={"tool":"pivotTable","params":{"input":"households","fields":"county","pivot":"year","value":"income","statistic":"MEAN"}}
</code></pre> <p>Field names come from the pivot values. A number, or true and false, follows the pivot field's name (<code>year2020</code>, <code>rate2_5</code> for 2.5, <code>temp_3</code> for −3). Text keeps its letters, digits and underscores, each run of other characters becoming one underscore (none right after an underscore) and underscores at its ends dropped (<code>North east</code> and <code>North_-east</code> give <code>North_east</code>), and follows the field's name when it would start with a digit or have nothing left. At most 64 characters of a value are kept; a name already taken, ignoring case, gets <code>_2</code>, <code>_3</code> and so on in the order of the pivot values. A record with no pivot value (or a number that isn't one, NaN) is an error: fill those in, or select the other records. The pivot field can have at most 1,000 values, and the table at most 10 million cells. Only selected records are used when the table has a selection, and the output is named <code>&lt;input&gt;_Pivot</code> unless <code>output</code> names it.</p> <p>The AI Assistant uses Pivot Table for questions such as "mean household income by county and year": it runs the tool with <code>county</code> as the row field, <code>year</code> as the pivot field and <code>MEAN</code>, then reads the table it names with <code>read_features</code>, a row per county.</p> Tissot's indicatrix on Robinson: equal circles, stretched and sheared by the projection. <p>Tissot's Indicatrix shows what the map's projection does to the Earth. It draws the same small circle repeated over a grid: by default circles of 500 km radius every 30° over the whole world. With Area: Map Extent it fills the view instead (the whole Earth on the globe), for a country or a state with a smaller spacing and radius.</p> <ul> <li>The circles are geodesic, in longitude/latitude. The map draws each one as the ellipse its projection makes of it, and redraws them when you change the projection: growing circles in Web Mercator, flattened ellipses of equal area in Mollweide.</li> <li> <p>On a flat map, each circle also records Tissot's parameters at its centre for the projection the map had when the tool ran:</p> <ul> <li><code>h</code> and <code>k</code>: scale along the meridian and the parallel;</li> <li><code>a</code> and <code>b</code>: the ellipse's semi-axes;</li> <li><code>area_scale</code>: <code>a·b</code>, which is 1 on an equal-area projection;</li> <li><code>max_angle</code>: the largest angular distortion ω in degrees, 0 on a conformal projection.</li> </ul> <p>They are measured against the WGS 84 ellipsoid. For a world projection the map draws on a sphere (Mollweide, Robinson…), they can be off by a few tenths of a percent. - Making a distortion map: color the circles by <code>area_scale</code> or <code>max_angle</code>.</p> </li> </ul> <p>A5 Grid and Aggregate Points to A5 use A5, a discrete global grid of pentagonal cells that all have the same area at a given resolution. A count per cell is therefore already a density, and stays comparable between regions, unlike a grid of cells that change size with latitude or the map's projection.</p> <ul> <li>A5 Grid makes the cells at a resolution (0–30; each step divides a cell into four: 5 is   about 33,000 km², 9 about 130 km², 12 about 2 km²) over the map's extent, an input layer's   polygons (or the extent of its points and lines), or the whole world. Cells: Overlapping   covers the area without gaps; Center keeps the cells whose centre is inside. A run makes at   most 200,000 cells.</li> <li>Aggregate Points to A5 counts a point layer's points (its selection, when there is one) by the   cell holding them, and keeps only the cells that hold any. It adds <code>count</code> and   <code>density_per_km2</code>, and a numeric field's <code>_sum</code> and <code>_mean</code>, where a feature's value counts   once in each cell (a multipoint's, however many of its points fall there).</li> <li>Both write <code>a5</code> (the cell's 64-bit id in hex, as other A5 tools read it: DuckDB, PostgreSQL,   Python's <code>pya5</code>, R), <code>resolution</code> and <code>area_km2</code>. A cell across the antimeridian, or round a pole, is   split at ±180° into two parts of one polygon, each within −180° to 180°.</li> </ul>

Graduated colours in the Symbology pane, with a selection synced between map and table. <p>Select a layer in Layers and open Style › Symbology (or the Symbology pane):</p> <ul> <li>Single Symbol: one fill, outline and marker for every feature.</li> <li>Unique Values: one colour per distinct value of a field. If no field is chosen, the current   renderer's field is kept when available; otherwise the first available field is used, including   text fields. The Layers pane and layout legends list only the   classes that drawn features use (after the definition query and display filters);   <code>&lt;all other values&gt;</code> appears only when a drawn feature falls outside the classes and its symbol   is not fully transparent. A layout legend can leave it out with Hide other values (see   Layouts).</li> <li>Graduated Colors: 1 to 12 classes of a numeric field by natural breaks (Jenks), equal   interval, quantile, geometric interval, standard deviation, arithmetic progression or head/tail   breaks (the Method list is in alphabetical order). Geometric interval makes class   widths grow by one ratio, for values piled up at the low end; for a field that reaches zero or   below, the minimum is taken as if it sat at a hundredth of the range, so the widths grow by   101^(1/classes). Standard deviation makes classes one deviation wide about the mean, and leaves   out a class no value falls in, so a skewed field can get fewer classes than asked. Arithmetic   progression makes class widths grow by one step (1, 2, 3… units), for more detail among the low   values. Head/tail breaks splits at the mean, then at the mean of the values above it, and so on   while those stay under 40% of the values they were taken from: for a few very large values among   many small ones (city sizes, incomes). It sets its own number of classes, at most the number   asked. Manual keeps the bounds you type: each class's upper bound is a number to edit or take out, Split the widest   class adds one, and the field's largest value closes the last class. If no field is   chosen, the current renderer's field is kept when it is numeric; otherwise the first numeric   field is used. If the layer has no numeric field, or the   switch fails for another reason, the pane shows why and keeps the current renderer. When numeric   fields exist, a field picker lets you try another one; the error stays visible if a retry fails.   A problem no field can fix, such as a definition query that keeps no features, needs fixing   first. Closing the pane or selecting another layer drops the retry.</li> <li>Proportional Symbols: point size grows with a numeric field, chosen as for Graduated Colors.</li> </ul> <p>A point's Shape is a circle, square, triangle, diamond or star, or a Character: any character of an installed font, picked under Font, as map signs often are (a symbol font's church, tree or mine). Its Size is the font's size, and the character sits as QGIS draws a font marker: centred across the point, its baseline half the font's ascent below it. A font the machine lacks draws the character in the label font, and a character no font here has draws a circle, so the point still shows. By command:</p> <pre><code>layer.marker={"layer":"Peaks","char":"▲","font":"DejaVu Sans","size":12}
</code></pre> <p>Rotation turns each point's marker by the angle in a number field, in degrees clockwise (Counterclockwise for angles as mathematics counts them), as wind arrows or the signs of a topographic map turn. Marks stack several markers at a point, bottom first, in place of the symbol's: a white disc under a sign, say, so it reads on any background. Each mark has its own colour, outline, shape, size and angle, and is shifted right (Offset right) and down (Offset down); its offset turns with its angle, as QGIS turns it. With the field turns a mark by the rotation field too, or keeps it still. A layer with classes can have marks for one class of its own, as line strokes can. By command, <code>layer.markerRotation</code> and the point layer's strokes of markers:</p> <pre><code>layer.markerRotation={"layer":"Signs","field":"angle","counterclockwise":true}
layer.lineStrokes={"layer":"Signs","strokes":[{"color":"#FFFFFF","markers":{"mark":"circle","size":14}},{"color":"#000000","markers":{"mark":"character","char":"▲","size":10,"offsetAlong":0}}]}
</code></pre>
